            <div class="prose max-w-none" x-show="lang === 'en'" x-cloak>
                <h2>This Week's Observation: Homogenization Is No Longer a Hidden Risk</h2>
<p>Over the past week, multiple overseas operations groups have been discussing the same phenomenon: <strong>AI-generated ad copy using identical prompts is producing increasingly divergent results on Meta and TikTok</strong>. While AI initially boosted efficiency in ad copywriting, both platforms and users are now "desensitizing"—the same selling points and phrasing have fatigued audiences, and platforms are actively suppressing repetitive creatives.</p>
<p>One quantifiable signal: a utility app team tested 20 AI-generated opening lines last week, with 15 of them underperforming historical averages by over 20%. Investigation revealed these 15 lines closely mirrored the core sentence structures of high-exposure creatives in their category.</p>
<p>This isn't an isolated case. Google Ads quietly updated its "Creative Diversity" report display logic this week, now showing advertisers the "similarity to competing ads" metric directly. The platform now possesses the technical capability to identify and flag homogenized creatives—traffic allocation is shifting.</p>
<h2>Key Observations: Three Dimensions Determining Whether Creatives Break Through</h2>
<h3>1. Platform Algorithm Level: Differentiation ≠ Complication</h3>
<p>Many operations teams share a misconception: to avoid homogenization, write longer, more winding copy stuffed with keywords. Results typically backfire. The platform's core criteria for differentiation is <strong>information increment</strong>, not word count. You need to provide specific information unavailable in other similar ads within limited character space.</p>
<p>Specifically, platform algorithms evaluate creatives based on:</p>
<ul>
<li>Whether there's a clear action trigger within the first 3 seconds</li>
<li>Whether the copy contains verifiable specific data or scenario descriptions</li>
<li>Overall similarity between the creative and historical creatives in that account</li>
</ul>
<blockquote>
<p>Practical insight: instead of writing "Limited-time offer" and "Register now" in every ad, anchor 5 different usage scenarios across 5 creatives. This signals to the platform that your product covers diverse needs.</p>
</blockquote>
<h3>2. Creative Strategy Level: Build "Comparison Baselines" Not "Imitation Baselines"</h3>
<p>AI-generated copy has an implicit tendency: <strong>it learns from the best-performing samples</strong>. When the entire industry uses AI to generate the "Slogan + Feature List + CTA" structure, AI treats this structure as the "correct answer." The result: you're imitating not a specific competitor, but the industry's average mediocrity.</p>
<p>The solution is <strong>proactively establishing new expression anchors</strong>. Specific approaches:</p>
<ol>
<li>Weekly, extract expression methods from high-engagement content outside your category (emotional short video pacing, lifestyle brand tone and style)</li>
<li>Use "reverse prompting" to have AI output below-average copy, then manually upgrade</li>
<li>Build an internal "copy gene bank" documenting classic phrases with different emotions and tones, for AI style constraints when calling</li>
</ol>
<h3>3. Team Collaboration Level: AI Handles Efficiency, Humans Handle "Pain Points"</h3>
<p>AI copy homogenization fundamentally stems from teams outsourcing "creative judgment" entirely. When AI output becomes the sole creative source, the team's aesthetic judgment and user empathy atrophy.</p>
<p>Recommendation: add one fixed question to every creative review: <strong>"Can only we write this copy this way?"</strong> If the answer is no, return to the Prompt layer for adjustment or switch to human creation.</p>
<h2>Actionable Recommendations: Two Things to Execute This Week</h2>
<p><strong>First, clean up the low-efficiency Prompt library.</strong> Review AI-generated copy underperforming the 30-day average, extract their Prompt templates, and analyze each problematic point. Common issues: missing differentiation constraints, lacking specific scenario descriptions, missing emotional positioning.</p>
<p><strong>Second, try "differentiation stress testing."</strong> Maintaining core selling points and target audience, randomly select 3 copy lines weekly and have team members blind test without viewing data: "Can you tell how this differs from competitors?" Copy scoring below 60% recognition should be adjusted.</p>
<p>Homogenization isn't AI's original sin—it's a usage problem. This week, transforming "differentiation" from a slogan into a quantifiable process will yield more authentic creative performance improvements.</p>
